{
  "teamId": 146,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Miami Marlins were a team that defied expectations from the moment the calendar turned. Armed with the best contact hitter in baseball in Luis Arraez, a young rotation anchored by Sandy Alcantara and Jesús Luzardo, and sluggers like Jorge Soler and Garrett Cooper providing the power, Miami scratched and clawed through April into a winning record before exploding in June with the best month in recent franchise memory. That 19-8 June stand was the heartbeat of the season — Bryan De La Cruz delivering clutch extra-base hits, Garrett Cooper launching home runs at pivotal moments, and the lineup finding ways to win games late and often.\n\nThe summer brought turbulence. July and August were genuinely difficult months, the kind that test a team's character. A.J. Puk became a liability in the bullpen, surrendering crushing home runs on back-to-back appearances, and the offense sputtered at key moments. Yet even through the dog days, Soler kept mashing — his home runs in early August helped the Marlins stay within striking distance of a playoff spot. Luis Arraez's gap-splitting triple in mid-August off a Yankees outfield featuring Aaron Judge was a reminder that this team still had life, even when the losses piled up.\n\nSeptember was a resurrection. The Marlins went 17-10 to close the regular season, with Jake Burger emerging as a force — his 32nd home run in September and a clutch single against the Mets capturing the defiant spirit of a team that simply refused to fold. They scraped into the Wild Card round and earned a date with the Philadelphia Phillies, the very team that had ended their 2022 postseason hopes. The stage was set for redemption.\n\nIt was not to be. Jesús Luzardo battled the Phillies' lineup in Game 1 but surrendered key doubles to Trea Turner, Alec Bohm, and Nick Castellanos in a losing effort. Game 2 brought more of the same, as Braxton Garrett couldn't hold the Phillies at bay and the Miami offense never found its footing on the big stage. The Wild Card sweep was a painful ending to a season that had promised so much more, but for a franchise still building toward sustained contention, 84 wins and a postseason berth was a genuine step forward.",
